      'The abdomen is the middle part of the trunk. Unlike the chest, which is enclosed by a rigid cage of ribs, most of the abdomen is surrounded by muscle, so it can expand when we eat, when gas builds up or during pregnancy. Above, it is separated from the chest by the domed diaphragm, which rises high under the ribs, so the upper abdominal organs such as the liver and spleen are partly hidden and protected by the lower ribs. Below, it opens freely into the pelvis at the pelvic brim, so the two cavities are really one continuous space.',
