      'The abdominal aorta enters the abdomen by passing behind the diaphragm at the level of T12 and runs down in front of the vertebrae, slightly to the left, until it divides into the two common iliac arteries at about L4, roughly level with the umbilicus. Its branches can be grouped by direction. Three unpaired branches go forwards to the gut: the coeliac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery and the inferior mesenteric artery. Paired branches go sideways to organs that come in pairs (the adrenals, kidneys and gonads) and backwards to the body wall (the lumbar arteries).',
